==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ja|改善}} (''kaizen'' かいぜん), from {{der|en|ltc|-}} [[改善]] (''kój-dʒjén'') (compare {{cog|cmn|-}} [[改善|''gǎishàn'' 改善]]), from {{der|en|och|-}} [[改善]] (''*qˁəʔ-ɡenʔ'' "to correct errors"), from [[改]] ("to change") + [[善]] ("good").

Introduced to English in 1959 by {{w|Boyé Lafayette De Mente}} in his book ''Japanese Etiquette and Ethics in Business''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkaɪˌzən/|/ˈkaɪˌzɛn/}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪzən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[Japanese]] [[business]] [[practice]] of [[continuous]] [[improvement]] in [[performance]] and [[productivity]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Continuous improvement generally.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 June 28|author=Sir Michael Holden|title=Comment: 'There will be more Nunehams': We need some serious '''''Kaizen'''... and we need it now|journal=RAIL|issue=986|page=3|text=This is a big mistake, because Japanese railways were largely built using British equipment and technology. But what they have done since is ruthlessly improve and refine it, using '''Kaizen''' principles.}}

